<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "https://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    Entitlements for the NativeAOT core.

    Keep Vault deliberately does not use the App Sandbox.

    The sandbox and this app's own integrity chain turned out to be mutually
    exclusive on macOS. Only a process that owns its sandbox is served an open,
    save or print panel; the core cannot own one, because the launcher verifies
    it and then replaces itself with it, so the core runs as an inheriting
    image. Delegating panels to a helper bundle does not work either: a
    sandboxed process cannot start one, neither by spawning it, which hangs in
    libsecinit before main, nor through LaunchServices, which reports success
    and starts nothing. Making the core the main executable would restore the
    panels but would put the core inside the bundle seal, and re-sealing
    rewrites it — which invalidates the hybrid RSA-PSS/ML-DSA-87 signature over
    the core that the launcher checks before running it.

    Between losing that signature and losing the sandbox, the signature is worth
    more: it is what makes tampering with the shipped binary detectable, and it
    is verified before a single instruction of the core executes. The sandbox
    would have limited the damage of a compromise; the signature chain is what
    prevents one.

    What remains in force: hardened runtime with library validation, the pinned
    Apple Team ID, the launcher's pre-execution verification, the dual
    SHA3-512/Skein-1024 manifests, the hybrid RSA-PSS + ML-DSA-87 signatures
    over every executable component, descriptor-based symlink-safe file access,
    and mlock'ed key material.

    The core declares no capability at all. Reading a printed key sheet's QR
    code needs the camera, and camera access under the hardened runtime is
    granted per process, so it lives in the separate scanner helper: the core
    holds the archive keys and runs for as long as the app is open, and letting
    it look through the camera for that whole time buys nothing.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict/>
</plist>
